     ttccppdduummpp prints out the headers of packets on a network interface that
     match the boolean _e_x_p_r_e_s_s_i_o_n.  You must have read access to _/_d_e_v_/_b_p_f.

     The options are as follows:

     --AA        Print each packet in ASCII.  If the --ee option is also speci‐
               fied, the link-level header will be included.  The smaller of
               the entire packet or _s_n_a_p_l_e_n bytes will be printed.

     --aa        Attempt to convert network and broadcast addresses to names.

     --BB _f_i_l_d_r_o_p
               Configure the drop action specified by _f_i_l_d_r_o_p to be used when
               the filter expression matches a packet.  The actions are:

                     ppaassss     Matching packets are accepted and captured.
                     ccaappttuurree  Matching packets are dropped and captured.
                     ddrroopp     Matching packets are dropped but not captured.

               The default action is ppaassss.

     --cc _c_o_u_n_t  Exit after receiving _c_o_u_n_t packets.

     --DD _d_i_r_e_c_t_i_o_n
               Select packets flowing in the specified _d_i_r_e_c_t_i_o_n.  Valid di‐
               rections are: iinn and oouutt.  The default is to accept packets
               flowing in any direction.

     --dd        Dump the compiled packet-matching code in a human readable form
               to standard output and stop.

     --dddd       Dump packet-matching code as a C program fragment.

     --dddddd      Dump packet-matching code as decimal numbers preceded with a
               count.

     --EE [_e_s_p_a_l_g:]_e_s_p_k_e_y
               Try to decrypt RFC 4835 ESP (Encapsulating Security Payload)
               traffic using the specified hex key _e_s_p_k_e_y.  Supported algo‐
               rithms for _e_s_p_a_l_g are: aaeess112288, aaeess112288--hhmmaacc9966, bblloowwffiisshh,
               bblloowwffiisshh--hhmmaacc9966, ccaasstt, ccaasstt--hhmmaacc9966, ddeess33, ddeess33--hhmmaacc9966, ddeess and
               ddeess--hhmmaacc9966.  The algorithm defaults to aaeess112288--hhmmaacc9966.  This op‐
               tion should be used for debugging only, since the key will show
               up in ps(1) output.

     --ee        Print the link-level header on each dump line.

     --FF _f_i_l_e   Use _f_i_l_e as input for the filter expression.  Any additional
               expressions given on the command line are ignored.

     --ff        Print “foreign” internet addresses numerically rather than sym‐
               bolically.  This option is intended to get around serious brain
               damage in Sun's yp server — usually it hangs forever translat‐
               ing non-local internet numbers.

     --II        Print the interface on each dump line.

     --ii _i_n_t_e_r_f_a_c_e
               Listen on _i_n_t_e_r_f_a_c_e.  If unspecified, ttccppdduummpp searches the sys‐
               tem interface list for the lowest numbered, configured “up” in‐
               terface (excluding loopback).  Ties are broken by choosing the
               earliest match.  _i_n_t_e_r_f_a_c_e may be either a network interface or
               a USB interface, for example _u_s_b_0.

